{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfBTRqFzi5kPirCWQGjiU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've submitted the episode, and I picked index 26 (Mg 0.24 / Sb 0.76), the most Sb-rich composition in the library. It had the highest EQE I measured, about 1.1e-3 %. Every measured value was tiny, as expected for wide-gap oxides under 2.7 eV light.\n\nI used all 3 merit measurements and 3 of the 5 XRD scans:\n\n| Index | Mg / Sb | EQE (%) | XRD |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 4 | 0.82 / 0.18 | not measured | Single cubic phase |\n| 13 | 0.62 / 0.38 | about 0 (−6.5e-5) | Mostly the cubic phase, weak extra peaks |\n| 23 | 0.33 / 0.67 | 4.3e-4 | Adds peaks that look like MgSb2O6 |\n| 26 | 0.24 / 0.76 | 1.1e-3 | not measured |\n\n- **Structure:** XRD shows one cubic phase on the Mg-rich side (peaks at Q ≈ 2.78, 4.55, 5.33). Toward the Sb-rich end, peaks at Q ≈ 1.91 and 3.22 appear, which likely belong to MgSb2O6.\n- **Trend:** EQE rose steadily with Sb content across the measured points, so the Sb-rich end is the best bet. I rejected the Mg-rich side (indices 0–10) without measuring its EQE, based on its cubic phase and that trend.\n- **Caveat:** with only three merit measurements, the other 24 compositions have no EQE values. I can't rule out an untested composition doing better, though the trend makes one on the Mg-rich side unlikely."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":560,"cache_read_input_tokens":8233,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":560},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1c59228c-5705-42c6-9430-567f89e04a2e","uuid":"3609cd81-94f5-4f0c-958e-82a07bb4bea1","timestamp":"2026-09-18T19:28:02.473Z","request_id":"req_011CfBTRpu